WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.675
Coverage gaps don't announce themselves,
and there are four ways they stay quiet.

2
00:00:04.675 --> 00:00:08.361
Some sources are never connected:
Azure resource logs aren't

3
00:00:08.361 --> 00:00:11.825
collected until each resource
has a diagnostic setting.

4
00:00:11.825 --> 00:00:16.623
Some connected sources aren't flowing:
Microsoft says the AWS connector's

5
00:00:16.623 --> 00:00:20.950
green status means a collection
rule exists, not that data arrived.

6
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.738
Some collected data is never analyzed.

7
00:00:23.738 --> 00:00:26.245
And some sources are
switched off on purpose;

8
00:00:26.245 --> 00:00:30.513
CISA calls tampering with logging
well-documented threat actor behavior.

9
00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:36.198
In the 2023 Exchange Online intrusion, a
federal victim caught it with its own alert

10
00:00:36.198 --> 00:00:41.637
on mailbox-access events that, at the time,
came only with a premium audit license.

11
00:00:41.637 --> 00:00:44.763
Agencies without those events
had nothing to look at.

12
00:00:44.763 --> 00:00:47.333
None of these gaps shows
up in what arrived,

13
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:50.638
only in comparing what arrived
with what was expected.

14
00:00:50.638 --> 00:00:57.978
That's why M-26-14 tells agencies to use
CDM and asset data to confirm log coverage,

15
00:00:57.978 --> 00:01:03.650
and why BOD 23-01 calls asset
visibility a basic pre-condition.

16
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.020
The inventory is the denominator.
